WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.250
Adam stores two numbers per parameter.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.970
The first follows the
gradient with its sign.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.610
The second follows its squared magnitude,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.970
so large recent gradients
increase the denominator.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.970
We use beta one zero point nine and
beta two zero point nine nine nine.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.250
Both buffers start at zero.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.030
That initialization pulls
early averages toward zero,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.970
so we correct them using the
number of completed updates.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.920
The first update is t equals one.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.350
A batch of thirty two increments t once,
just like a one row update.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.830
It is not the count of examples visited.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.720
Our implementation uses no
weight decay or AMSGrad,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.530
and keeps epsilon outside the square root.

00:00:44.608 --> 00:00:49.425
The real hour still begins with gradient
negative forty eight point seven four

00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:50.618
seven three.

00:00:51.228 --> 00:00:53.658
The first moment stores one tenth of it,

00:00:53.728 --> 00:00:56.618
about negative four point
eight seven four seven.

00:00:56.988 --> 00:01:00.078
The second stores one
thousandth of its square,

00:01:00.468 --> 00:01:02.938
about two point three seven six three.

00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:08.628
Dividing by zero point one and zero point
zero zero one restores the first gradient

00:01:08.628 --> 00:01:09.758
and its square.

00:01:10.228 --> 00:01:13.738
The square root is approximately
its absolute magnitude.

00:01:14.208 --> 00:01:16.338
Their ratio is almost negative one.

00:01:16.908 --> 00:01:21.748
Rate zero point zero one therefore
increases this coordinate by about zero

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:22.898
point zero one,

00:01:23.268 --> 00:01:25.798
much less than the first SGD movement.

00:01:26.328 --> 00:01:27.818
Across all coordinates,

00:01:28.028 --> 00:01:31.948
the resulting forecast is one hundred
and forty four point zero two four nine

00:01:31.948 --> 00:01:32.838
rentals.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:39.777
The source advances the update counter,
then accumulates both buffers.

00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:44.577
These shortened names correspond
to explicit configuration fields.

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.277
At the second real hour update,

00:01:47.547 --> 00:01:50.837
the gradient is about minus
forty seven point six five.

00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:55.337
The stored first moment is
about minus nine point one five,

00:01:55.787 --> 00:01:58.137
and the second is about
four point six four.

00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:00.417
At update two,

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:05.777
each correction denominator is one minus
its beta squared, as shown in the code.

00:02:06.227 --> 00:02:09.777
Reusing the first step correction
would give the wrong calculation.

00:02:10.887 --> 00:02:14.947
The hour eight coefficient
becomes just under two hundredths.

00:02:14.947 --> 00:02:18.898
Recomputing every coordinate gives a
second forecast of about one hundred and

00:02:18.898 --> 00:02:21.337
forty four point two six rentals.

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:23.627
The notes retain the exact values,

00:02:23.627 --> 00:02:27.157
and the independent scalar
trace agrees with the code.

00:02:29.192 --> 00:02:30.802
Now change the case.

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:35.372
A scalar parameter starts at zero
and receives gradient plus two,

00:02:35.372 --> 00:02:38.042
then minus one, at rate zero point one.

00:02:38.172 --> 00:02:42.102
The first step takes it to
approximately negative zero point one.

00:02:42.152 --> 00:02:47.102
After the sign reversal, the first moment
is still positive zero point zero eight.

00:02:47.452 --> 00:02:52.252
Correcting that positive average and
dividing by a positive magnitude moves the

00:02:52.252 --> 00:02:57.002
parameter farther down, to negative
zero point one two six six three four.

00:02:57.572 --> 00:03:02.182
Restarting the buffers would instead react
to the latest negative gradient alone.

00:03:02.752 --> 00:03:04.342
The retained history matters.

00:03:05.052 --> 00:03:09.332
This changing gradient check is more
informative than testing only a repeated

00:03:09.332 --> 00:03:09.712
gradient,

00:03:09.712 --> 00:03:13.992
because it exposes whether the
implementation actually carries state

00:03:13.992 --> 00:03:14.982
between updates.

00:03:17.512 --> 00:03:20.842
Epsilon becomes important
for a tiny gradient.

00:03:21.272 --> 00:03:26.342
With gradient one times ten to the
negative ten and rate zero point one,

00:03:26.412 --> 00:03:30.630
our first corrected update is
approximately negative zero point zero

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:34.022
zero zero nine nine zero zero nine nine.

00:03:34.352 --> 00:03:38.342
Putting epsilon inside the square
root would produce a different number.

00:03:38.652 --> 00:03:41.522
A direct arithmetic check
protects this convention.

00:03:41.872 --> 00:03:43.282
At the same rate,

00:03:43.412 --> 00:03:48.092
Adam moved less than SGD on the opening
hour because it normalized gradient

00:03:48.092 --> 00:03:49.022
magnitude.

00:03:49.412 --> 00:03:52.902
That is not proof that it is
slower in every useful comparison.

00:03:53.832 --> 00:03:59.242
Our equal allowance pilot selects Adam
rate one for the unscaled rental target.

00:03:59.552 --> 00:04:02.572
Compare measured behavior with
declared tuning allowances,

00:04:02.572 --> 00:04:04.542
and keep the moments,

00:04:04.752 --> 00:04:08.382
counter and numerical convention
in every continuation record.

